# ============================================================================
# Morse Torch: a torch first, and a way to talk with light second.
#
#   One tap on the big button and the torch is on: steady, or flashing at the
#   pace set in Settings. "Screen light" turns the whole screen white, with a
#   dimmer. "Tell it" listens: say "torch on", "flash", "screen", "off", or
#   "send" and a message.
#
#   "Morse" is the clever half. Type or say a message and Send flashes it on
#   the torch, showing the dots and dashes as they go. "Watch for light" finds
#   whatever is flashing in the camera's picture, shows the dots and dashes as
#   they arrive, writes the letters, and reads the message out when the light
#   goes quiet. Send while watching and a mirror will show you both halves.
#
#   What it uses, all through Vml(...):
#     Torch       just a torch            Flash        a timed on/off pattern
#     Listen      speech to text          Speak        text to speech
#     WatchLight  "the light went on/off, and how long the last state lasted"
#     Brightness  the screen's own brightness, for the screen light
#   In a browser Flash lights the whole screen, which a phone reads as well.
#
#   The form is four pages laid over each other: Page() at the bottom shows
#   one and hides the rest. The Morse itself is Lua too: Pattern() turns text
#   into milliseconds on and off, and Read() turns flashes back into text.
#
#   Made with Visualised.
# ============================================================================

